{"section_id":"MA-S1","heading_text":"","section_markdown":"## Motivation: efficiency and cross‑passage inference challenges in existing rerankers.

Existing reranking architectures face a fundamental efficiency challenge when extending from single-passage scoring to cross-passage inference. In cross-passage inference, the model must attend across multiple retrieved passages simultaneously, so inference cost scales with the number of passages fed into the attention mechanism rather than remaining constant per passage. This scaling behavior makes cross-passage reranking substantially more expensive at inference time than listwise or pointwise alternatives that score each passage independently. The present setup addresses this by filtering candidate documents before they enter the reranking stage: a document is retained for downstream inference only when its size field is not None and its chunk identifier matches the corresponding query chunk identifier. This pre-filtering step reduces the passage set that the cross-passage attention layer must process, directly targeting the efficiency bottleneck without altering the inference architecture itself.

